response:
Let's use the AM-HM inequality. The inequality states that the arithmetic mean of a set of positive numbers is always at least as large as the harmonic mean (\textit{harmonic mean} refers to the reciprocal of the arithmetic mean of the reciprocals)
We can express the harmonic mean more precisely, and let that be our key tool.
\begin{align*}
\frac{\frac{1}{2} (a + b + b + c)}{\frac{1}{a + b} + \frac{1}{b + c}} &\ge \frac{n}{\frac{n}{a + b} + \frac{n}{b + c}}
\end{align*}
Noting that
\begin{align} &\frac{1}{a + b} + \frac{1}{b + c} \\ &= \frac{1}{a + b} + \frac{1}{b + c} \end{align}
and simplifying, we get
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{a + b} + \frac{1}{b + c} &\ge \frac{2 (a + b + 2b + c)}{(b + c) (a + b)}
\end{align*}
But we are given the constraint that $a + b + c = 5$ so we can express
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{a + b} + \frac{1}{b + c} &= \frac{2 (a + b + 2b + c)}{(b + c) (a + b)} \\ &= \frac{10}{b (a + b)}
\end{align*}
In order to isolate $\frac{1}{a + b} + \frac{1}{b + c}$ as a function of $b$ alone, we will eliminate $a$, making use of our given constraint.
\begin{align*}
b = 5 - a - c &= \frac{(a + b) - (b + c)}{2} \\ &= \frac{5 - c - c}{2} \\ &= \frac{5 - 2c}{2}
\end{align*}
Thus, plugging this into the denominator of $\frac{10}{b (a + b)}$ gives us
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{a + b} + \frac{1}{b + c} &= \frac{10}{b (a + b)} \\ &= \frac{10}{\left(\frac{5 - 2c}{2}\right)(5 - c)}
\end{align*}
Noting that
\begin{align*}
