Of rationalizing this expression, the first step would be to change the order of the square roots in the denominator. To achieve this, we try to find two consecutive multiplication coefficients that give us the desired expression. We find that
\[
\sqrt{8+\sqrt{32+\sqrt{768}}}=2\cos\frac{\sqrt{35-2\sqrt{768}}}{16}\,.
\]
We now simplify the expression and begin to rationalize to obtain the desired result. With some more algebra, it follows that
\begin{align*}
8+\sqrt{32+\sqrt{768}}
&=8+\sqrt{32+\sqrt{2^4\cdot3\cdot16}}\\
&=8+\sqrt{32+4\sqrt{3}\sqrt{16}}\\
&=8+\sqrt{32+16\sqrt{3}}\\
&=(4+\sqrt{3})^2+(4-\sqrt{3})^2.
\end{align*}
So we now have
\begin{align*}
2\cos\frac{\sqrt{35-2\sqrt{768}}}{16}
&=2\cdot\frac{(4+\sqrt{3})+2(4-\sqrt{3})}{(4+\sqrt{3})^2-(4-\sqrt{3})^2}\\
&=2\cdot\frac{8}{35-32}\\
&=16.
\end{align*}
Plugging into the formula, we have that $(a,b)=(16,35)$. <answer>
